steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;EM&bull;Scope Capture Collections}

      So far, we've worked with pre-populated collections of **EM&bull;Scope** captures.&thinsp; Now you'll create your own&thinsp;!!

      Any EMS **bucket** can hold _one_ capture collection (named `emscope-captures`), alongside related source files.

      Our familiar {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} captures {[dc,1]} reside within `em.benchmark.coremark` &ndash; as does {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} itself.

      Individual `em.mark.*` _activity_ buckets (such as {[dc,2]} and {[dc,3]}) follow this same pattern.

      Clicking on any `emscope-captures` element {[dc,1]} opens this capture collection within **EM&bull;Scope**.

      <div class="em-info">

      This tour uses the special `zlocal` package as a private sandbox for experimenting with **EM&bull;Scope**.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$build]&thinsp;Prepare the Benchmark}

      The capture collection you'll soon create will benchmark al&shy;ternative forms of "waiting" on a particular MCU.

      <div class="em-info">

      This tour's (portable) examples chose the `ti.cc23xx` distro &ndash; but work equally well with other target MCUs.

      </div>

      Our first benchmark uses the {[cu]BusyWaitA} activity from the prior tour {[tr,104/03/03]} &ndash; to wait for 50&thinsp;ms, once per second.

      We've copied {[cu]BusyWaitC} {[tr,104/03/06]} into our own compos&shy;ite [{[cu]BusyWait50C}] which explicitly binds {[cn]duration_us} {[dc,1]}.

      Running {[cu]BusyWait50C}, **Logic 2** confirms the program's ten periodic events {[dc,2]} which each spend ~50&thinsp;ms {[dc,3]} waiting.

      <div class="em-info">

      To "play along at home", create your _own_ private copy of `BusyWait50C.em.ts` within the `zlocal` package.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,$look]&thinsp;Connect and Verify}

      With {[cu]BusyWait50C} built and loaded, we'll now observe its _current consumption_ using Nordic's [PPK2](https://www.nordicsemi.com/Products/Development-hardware/Power-Profiler-Kit-2) analyzer.

      <div class="em-info">

      The **PPK** operates as a **Source** or **Ampere** meter &ndash; the form&shy;er powering the DUT at a selected voltage.

      </div>

      In this setup, we've disconnected the debugger adapter from the DUT and then connected the **PPK** as shown {[dc,1]}.

      Using Nordic's desktop app, select  **Source Meter** at 3V0 and enable DUT power _before_ you start recording.

      A trace like {[dc,2]} should appear, with a large inrush at reset followed by ten WORK-SLEEP activity cycles.

      Zooming in to one WORK burst {[dc,3]} shows sustained cur&shy;rent during busy-wait, followed by a return to deep SLEEP.

